A("H. E. Bates", SEC, "1905", "1974", "Rushden, Northamptonshire, England",
  "British; the Northamptonshire novelist of the country and the war",
  "Novelist and short-story writer; the author of The Poacher and Fair Stood the Wind for France.",
  "Born Herbert Ernest Bates at Rushden, Northamptonshire, on 4 June 1905; he left school at eighteen for journalism and published his first novel in 1926. The Poacher (1935) and A House of Women (1936) made him the novelist of the Northamptonshire country, and Fair Stood the Wind for France (1944) the most read novel of the airman in the war; his short stories and his creation of the Larkin family followed after the war. He died at Canterbury on 29 January 1974.",
  "H. E. Bates is the novelist of the English country and of the airman's war: The Poacher carried the country novel of the county into the 1930s in a prose of the field and the season, A House of Women made the household of women a novel of the decade, and Fair Stood the Wind for France is the most read novel of the airman behind the lines.",
  "The prose of the field, the season and the weather, charged but never purple; the country county and its poacher, its farm and its market; the household of women and the man who enters it; the airman's France in the novel of escape; the short story of the single observed moment.",
  "The English country and the vanishing rural world; the poacher and the field as the moral centre; the household of women and the war; the airman behind the lines and the occupied country; the county as the world entire.",
  "H. E. Bates is the novelist of the English country and of the airman's war: The Poacher carried the country novel into the 1930s and Fair Stood the Wind for France is the most read novel of the airman behind the lines.",
  infl=["Thomas Hardy", "Anton Chekhov", "Guy de Maupassant", "Edward Thomas", "the Northamptonshire fields"],
  alt=["Herbert Ernest Bates", "H.E. Bates"],
  also=["The Two Sisters (1926)", "Love for Lydia (1952)", "The Darling Buds of May (1958)",
        "collected short stories from 1932"]),

