  "British; the short-story writer of the strange and the everyday",
  "Short-story writer and poet; the author of Adam and Eve and Pinch Me and The Field of Mustard.",
  "Born Alfred Edgar Coppard at Folkestone on 4 January 1878; he left school at nine and worked as a clerk and a machine salesman until the stories of Adam and Eve and Pinch Me (1921) made him a writer. The Black Dog and Other Stories (1923) and The Field of Mustard (1926) fixed his manner of the strange in the everyday, and his Collected Tales of 1948 made him one of the masters of the English short story; he died at Oxford on 13 January 1957.",
  "A. E. Coppard is among the masters of the English short story between the wars: his tales of the strange in the everyday - the black dog, the field of mustard, the fair and the funeral - carried the wonder-tale into the village and the market, and his verse of the country is the companion to the stories.",
  "The short story of the single strange turn in the everyday; the village, the fair and the market of the southern counties; the prose of the spoken rhythm and the sudden image; the ballad and the country lyric in verse; the deadpan of the teller who does not explain.",
  "The strange in the everyday; the village and the fair of the southern country; poverty and the clerk's life self-taught; the ballad and the wonder-tale as the form of the modern story; the unexplained as the moral centre.",
  "A. E. Coppard is among the masters of the English short story between the wars: his tales of the strange in the everyday carried the wonder-tale into the village and the market, and his Collected Tales fixed his place in the form.",
