A("Sean O'Faolain", MAJ, "1900", "1991", "Cork, Ireland",
  "Irish; the short-story writer, the novelist of Bird Alone and the editor of The Bell",
  "Short-story writer, novelist, critic and editor; the founder of The Bell and the biographer of the revival.",
  "Born Sean Proinsias O Faolain at Cork on 22 February 1900; he fought in the IRA and the civil war, and published Midsummer Night Madness (1932), The Saint and Mary Kate (1932) and Bird Alone (1936). As editor of The Bell from 1940 and as critic and biographer of the revival he shaped Irish letters for two decades; he died at Dublin on 20 April 1991.",
  "Sean O'Faolain is the central figure of Irish letters between the war and the republic: Midsummer Night Madness and Bird Alone made the short story and the novel of the civil war and the censorship, The Bell gave Irish writing its magazine for the 1940s, and his criticism of the revival fixed the terms of the national question in literature.",
  "The short story of the civil war and the censorship in the plain ironic sentence; the novel of the young man against the church and the state; the criticism of the revival as the argument with the fathers; the editor's prose of the standard and the quarrel; the biography of the national leader as the study of the myth.",
  "The civil war and the republic that followed; the censorship and the church; the Irish short story and its magazine; the revival and the argument with the fathers; the national question as the question of the writer.",
  "Sean O'Faolain is the central figure of Irish letters between the war and the republic: Bird Alone and Midsummer Night Madness made the fiction of the civil war and the censorship, and The Bell gave Irish writing its magazine of the 1940s.",
  infl=["Daniel Corkery", "Anton Chekhov", "George Moore", "the Irish revival", "Frank O'Connor"],
  alt=["Sean Proinsias O Faolain", "Seán Ó Faoláin"],
  also=["The Bell (magazine, editor from 1940)", "biographies of the national leaders from 1938"]),

