  "Born Kate O'Brien at Limerick on 23 July 1897; she worked as a governess and a journalist before Without My Cloak (1931) won the James Tait Black prize. The Anteroom (1934), Mary Lavelle (1936) - banned in Ireland - and The Last of Summer (1943) made her the novelist of the Irish middle class, the convent and the censored desire; she died at Faversham, Kent, on 13 August 1974.",
  "Kate O'Brien is the novelist of the Irish middle class and of the censored conscience: Without My Cloak made the Limerick family a chronicle of the century, Mary Lavelle carried the convent-bred woman into the forbidden love that the Irish state banned, and The Last of Summer is the finest of her novels of the return to Ireland.",
