A("Samuel Beckett", MAJ, "1906", "1989", "Foxrock, Dublin, Ireland",
  "Irish; the early prose and verse of Murphy and the Joyce essay",
  "Novelist, poet and critic; the author of More Pricks Than Kicks, Echo's Bones and Murphy before the war.",
  "Born Samuel Barclay Beckett at Foxrock, Dublin, on 13 April 1906; he read Romance languages at Trinity College Dublin and taught in Paris, where the essay 'Dante...Bruno. Vico..Joyce' (1929) placed him in the Joyce circle. More Pricks Than Kicks (1934), Echo's Bones and Other Precipitates (1935) and Murphy (1938) are the Dublin and London books of the 1930s; the war, the Resistance and the French trilogy followed. He received the Nobel prize in 1969 and died in Paris on 22 December 1989.",
  "Samuel Beckett is the central writer of the twentieth century and his English books of the 1930s are the first act of it: the Joyce essay stated the poetics of the wake, More Pricks Than Kicks and Echo's Bones gave the comic elegy of Belacqua, and Murphy is the novel of the mind in the body that the later prose would dissolve.",
  "The comic elegiac sentence of the learned allusion and the bodily joke; the paralysis of the will as the plot; the Dublin and London of the young intellectual; the verse of the precipitate and the echo; the prose that thinks against its own speaker.",
  "The mind and the body and the little world of the former; Joyce and the poetics of the wake; the comic and the elegiac in the same sentence; paralysis and the refusal of the will; the war and the Resistance as the break of the career.",
  "Samuel Beckett is the central writer of the twentieth century: the English books of the 1930s - the Joyce essay, More Pricks Than Kicks, Echo's Bones and Murphy - are the first act of the career that the French trilogy and the plays would complete.",
  infl=["James Joyce", "Dante", "René Descartes", "Arnold Geulincx", "the Dublin of Trinity College"],
  alt=["Samuel Barclay Beckett"],
  also=["Waiting for Godot (1952, in French)", "Molloy (1951, in French)", "The Unnamable (1953)",
        "Endgame (1957)", "Watt (1953)"]),

