  "Born Patrick Kavanagh at Inniskeen, County Monaghan, on 21 October 1904, the son of a small farmer and shoemaker; he farmed the stony land until The Ploughman and Other Poems (1936) and the autobiography The Green Fool (1938) made him a writer in Dublin. The Great Hunger (1942), the long poem of the clay and the celibate farmer, is his masterpiece; he died in Dublin on 30 November 1967.",
  "Patrick Kavanagh is the Monaghan poet of the Irish twentieth century: The Great Hunger made the clay, the potato and the celibate farmer the matter of the long poem and answered the Ireland of the revival with the Ireland of the parish, and his lyrics of the ploughman are the plain voice of the small farm.",
